Enables you to view effects of workload against single/multiple databases, and it makes recommendations to alter the physical design structure of SQL Server databases.
Goal of DTA recommendations is to enable the query server to execute the query in the least amount of time.
Use DTA tool to troubleshoot issues caused by the existing indexing strategy and to perform what-if analysis on the databases.
The database administrator needs to specify an initial log file size equal to 25% of the initial application database size.
Set SQLServer:Databases:Percent Log used to trigger a SQL Server performance condition alert that starts a transaction log backup and sends you an email.
Active portion of the log cannot be truncated/removed by shrinking.
These are used to discover what is preventing log truncation.
You must consider whether and how the database will grow beyond the initial space you allocate.
Use SSMS or the CREATE DATABASE if you do not want the database files to grow larger than when they were created.
This approach lets you grow the database without filling up the disk drive.
If the database should not expand beyond its initial size, set maximum growth size to zero.
A proportional fill strategy across all files within each filegroup.
When all the files in the filegroup are full, the database engine automatically expands one file at a time in a round-robin manner to allow for more data.
Create files/filegroups on as many different local physical disks as possible.
Before a transaction reads/modifies data, it must protect itself from the effects of another transaction modifying the data.
No transaction can be granted a lock that would conflict with the mode of a lock already granted on that data to another transaction.
When a transaction modifies some data, the transaction holds the lock protecting the modification until the end of the transaction.
Locks are managed internally by the database lock manager.
