Young and old, healthy and hobbled, they filed into the chapel in the Boston Archdiocese’s pastoral center Thursday, from a line that stretched outside the main entrance and down a double line along the building.
Word spread quickly this week that the Italian Capuchin monk’s heart – a “first-degree relic” – was coming to the Archdiocese, first to Lowell and the North End, then to Braintree on Thursday and Boston on Friday. It was the first time Padre Pio’s heart had been taken outside Italy.
The Archdiocese said 5,000 came from as far away as New Hampshire and Rhode Island to seek the blessing of a saint known for his piety and famous for his stigmata – mystical marks in his palms, feet and side that resembled the nail and spear wounds Jesus suffered when he was crucified.
