Last time we went into detail about how part-time 4WD works and why it’s beneficial to have all four wheels powering the car in low-traction situations. Today, we tackle full-time 4WD, as well as All-Wheel Drive (aka AWD).
Why would you want 4WD on full-time, instead of just turning it on when needed? The primary reason is improved handling.
Let’s say you’re driving on a road that has segments of ice and snow but is mostly dry. Instead of having to engage and disengage your part-time 4WD when you hit and leave those slick spots, with the four wheels going all the time, you don’t have to think about it.
