North End pilgrims walk 32 kilometres
Pilgrims walk, sing hymns and say the rosary. North End Catholics walked to Welwyn, Sask.
The pilgrimage organized by Our Lady of the Rosary Roman Catholic Church in Winnipeg and Our Lady Fatima Chapel in Welwyn started on Sept. 2. This is the 15th year North Enders have taken part in the spiritual journey and according to a press release, “”it has become one of the most well-established traditional Catholic pilgrimages in Western Canada.””
“”The reason why we went here is to meet with other traditional-minded Catholics and to give a little bit of a boost to our faith,”” Gerspacher said. “”The spirit of prayer that we had through the whole (walk) and overall there’s a great spirit of sharing and co-operation.””
Stokes said the pilgrimage becomes difficult after a while, but that a spiritual connection comes soon after.
You feel that you’re not alone.”” The pilgrims were back in Winnipeg on Sept. 4, where they had a big dinner and a bonfire to rest and share their experience with others.
